WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.320
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.433
and I'm musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.710
University's Fortunoff Video Archive for

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.240
Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.619
This video series is designed as a companion

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.780
to our album Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.299
which is the first volume in the archives

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.819
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.973
and poems performed by survivors in testimonies

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.069
given to the Fortunoff Archive over

00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:59.500
the last 40 years.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.879
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.623
Archives website, songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.879
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.139
Songs from Testimonies.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.319
Volume One was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.139
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble featuring

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.017
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.454
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.939
Camp on the accordion, keyboards and guitar,

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.029
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass, and myself

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.030
on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.955
The songs in this collection differ vastly in

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.873
terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.400
background, language, and many other aspects,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.609
but collectively they create a multidimensional

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.031
picture of Jewish life before

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.659
and during World War Two.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.730
Irene S. was born in 1925

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.043
in Brzezany or Berezhany, in Galicia,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.840
now in Ukraine, and was raised in Grudziadz,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.559
Poland.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.814
Irene was the prisoner of the Bialystok ghetto

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.569
and later of several concentration camps,

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.356
including Majdanek, Treblinka, and

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.150
Auschwitz.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.659
Irene was a slave laborer in Germany and

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.870
was liberated by the Americans in Kaunitz.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.847
By the time the war broke out, Irene was an

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.050
accomplished singer-songwriter.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.699
In her testimony, she recalls singing

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.513
existing songs and composing new ones

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.425
together with her young fellow prisoners from

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.029
Poland and the Soviet Union.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.089
While Irene's native language was Polish,

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.713
she co-wrote this song with a Russian

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.328
speaking boy as they were transported from

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.344
Bialystok Ghetto to Majdanek, by the

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.010
way of Treblinka.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.240
The song Manifesto of the Enslaved Children

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.813
was created in the genre of the heroic military

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.759
march in the minor key, which was

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.129
quite typical for the protest and resistance

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:46.130
songs of the era.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.340
It's, uh.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.620
Oh, I don't know if I could sing it

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:57.339
now.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.340
Just try.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:07.180
Thunderstorms of war embrace the world,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.660
And the workers’ blood is flowing.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.060
We, yesterday’s children of freedom,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.500
Are today’s tribe of slaves.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.860
Just a verse, and--

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.620
What does it mean?

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.860
This is in Russian that uh,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.120
a world wide

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.689
storm is embracing this world.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:37.920
And, uh,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.430
people's blood is being shed all, all over.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.469
And yesterday's free children

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.419
were today's.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.280
Were today's

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.349
war prisoners, prisoners of war,

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.470
or today's slaves, really.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.900
The best translation is, today we

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.489
are a bunch of slaves.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.420
And it goes on.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.920
We are broke-- we are

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.390
kept apart from the rest of the world and

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.170
locked in this ghetto.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.199
And we are beaten with the

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:19.389
Nazi

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:21.760
stick.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.749
But if today life was bad for

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.319
us, we are still hoping for a better

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.019
tomorrow. Well, all of
those songs were always

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.020
like this.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.500
Can you sing that part about the hope

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:36.740
that you had?

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:45.540
Locked up in the ghetto,
torn from the world,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.500
Lashed with the Nazi whip.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:55.180
But if today is hard and miserable,

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.660
We will wait for tomorrow;

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.180
If today is hard and miserable,

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.100
We will wait for a better tomorrow.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.609
The reason for it was written in Russian

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.717
was that the boy who wrote the words didn't

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.499
speak Yiddish and so we had

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.539
a choice of Polish or Russian.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.076
So we wrote it in Russian.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:38.540
Thunderstorms of war embrace the world,

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.540
And the workers’ blood is flowing.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.460
We, yesterday’s children of freedom,

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:52.620
Are today’s tribe of slaves.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.660
We, yesterday’s children of freedom,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.940
Are today’s tribe of slaves.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:21.260
Locked up in the ghetto,
torn from the world,

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.580
Lashed with the Nazi whip.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:31.300
But if today is hard and miserable,

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.660
We will wait for tomorrow;

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.500
If today is hard and miserable,

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:45.180
We will wait for a better tomorrow.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.940
Many people, when they think

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.400
of the ghettos in Eastern Europe,

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.190
mistakenly regard them

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.069
as being only

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.429
holding pens for the

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.540
death camps or antechambers

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.679
to the concentration camps.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:15.230
But in fact, these ghettos

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.410
were still communities

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.209
in their own right.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:23.299
On the one hand,

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.629
they were still shaped to

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.600
some degree or other by prewar values,

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:30.918
by prewar memories, by

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.629
prewar social customs and relationships.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.569
And on the other hand, they were

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:41.119
dealing with unprecedented

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:42.900
oppression.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.950
Now, in the prewar communities,

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:47.941
the Jews had newspapers, they

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.299
had radio.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.379
In the ghettos, access

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.144
to these forms of social interaction

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.979
and communication were terribly limited.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:02.869
And what took their place

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.299
to a very large degree

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:06.750
were songs.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:08.483
And singing became a basic,

00:08:09.525 --> 00:08:12.319
a medium,

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.689
a basic type

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.969
of ghetto interaction.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.890
Street singers were very popular,

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.460
just take a look at Jankele Herszkowicz

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.110
in the Lodz ghetto.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.666
People sang together.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:32.439
Singing together expressed

00:08:32.709 --> 00:08:34.740
hopes, fears.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.709
They express the trauma of loss.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.090
If you lost a child or a husband,

00:08:41.319 --> 00:08:43.208
a song could

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.439
not only express your own

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.520
injury, your own hurt,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.369
but the people who were singing

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.829
that song with you let you

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.300
know that they've suffered the

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:00.260
same loss and they

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:04.419
are experiencing what you're

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.610
experiencing.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.679
One of the key features of ghetto

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.559
life was the constant

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.410
contraction of the distance

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:17.139
between the individual

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:18.840
and the collective.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.999
What happens to me in the ghetto

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:24.459
will probably happen to us

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.949
and what happens to us

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.500
will probably happen to me.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:33.289
There were different kinds of ghetto

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.300
songs.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.580
One very common

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.149
kind of ghetto song was

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.426
the inverted prewar

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.069
lullaby or folk song,

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:49.519
a tune that everybody

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:50.990
knew, a lullaby that everybody

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.829
remembered, would be sung,

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.369
but with different words expressing

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.130
the stark contrast between

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.535
the before and the now.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.380
A lullaby like "Rozinkes Mit

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.662
Mandlen," "Raisins and Almonds,"

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:12.560
which mothers would sing to their children,

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:15.950
hoping that they would grow up

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.649
to be not only learned in Torah,

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.369
but a proficient and wealthy merchant.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.849
Lullabies which talk about how Daddy

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:28.340
is going to come home and he is going

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.479
to bring gifts for all of us.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:33.160
These words were now changed

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.099
to express the fact that what

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.410
what we have now is,

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.429
is, is absolutely different

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.229
from what we used to have.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:48.600
In one lullaby in the Lodz

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:51.430
ghetto, the words are

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.780
"nisht keyn rozinkes nisht keyn mandeln

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:56.980
tumbling der tate iz nisht gefarn handeln."

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.620
"We don't have raisins, we don't

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:00.790
have almonds.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.849
Our father did not go out to trade.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.100
In fact, our father is gone.

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:08.500
We may never see him again." So

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.650
this was one kind of ghetto song.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:13.180
Then there were beggar songs in

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:14.367
the Warsaw Ghetto.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.781
For example, "ikh, ikh gib nisht ab

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:19.460
di bone," "I'm not giving up my ration

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.760
card, I want to survive."

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:23.720
There were parodies,

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.060
sarcastic criticisms

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:31.049
of the Judenrat and the Jewish police.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:33.735
In the Vilna ghetto especially,

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.349
many of the songs were connected

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.630
to the cabaret and the theater.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.479
The Vilna Ghetto was, had many

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.999
gifted song writers like

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.260
Kasriel Broyde and Leyb Rozental.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:52.340
And the Cabaret,

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.170
the Ghetto Theater were very, very popular

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.269
and some of these songs

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.140
were very quickly picked up by everybody.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:03.979
A song like "Moyshe, halt zikh," sung

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.429
to an American ragtime beat, expressing

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:09.889
the hope that you've got to get out of

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.780
here. It's going to change.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:13.190
Just hold on.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:14.710
Don't get depressed.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.050
A song like "Pesha fon Resha,"

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:19.760
Pesha's a 17 year old girl.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:22.340
She's lost her parents, but

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:24.859
she's not going to give up hope.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.499
She can find her way

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:28.879
any, anywhere.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:30.769
She's not going to let herself

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:32.490
get depressed.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.300
There's a song, Yis, "Yisrolek,"

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.079
about a tough little kid who's lost

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:39.489
his mommy and daddy.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.459
But nonetheless, nobody's

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.469
going to push him around.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:47.700
And then finally, we have

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.340
the kind of song that Irene

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.159
Shapiro wrote, these are what

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.380
I would call part of the general

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.349
genre of partisan songs.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:01.149
They radiate defiance

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:03.010
and resistance.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.849
They stress the con, the

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:09.340
contrast between the

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:11.660
ghetto today and a

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:14.160
better life tomorrow.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.990
These are not songs of despair

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.230
or, or nostalgia.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.059
They're not particularly Jewish.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:26.569
Instead, they seek to leap

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.629
over the walls that separate

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.740
Jews from non-Jews and

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.340
reiterate faith in a better world

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.189
after the war is over.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.540
And it also reminds us of something

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.440
that is very often forgotten,

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.709
that in the war, the USSR

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.440
for Jews in the East European ghettos

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:51.760
was in fact the only hope.

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:54.620
Great Britain had betrayed the Jews,

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:55.980
with the white paper

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.540
ending its promises to the Zionist

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:01.420
movement. This happened in

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:04.700
1939. The United States was

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.020
indifferent and

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.940
there was a natural desire

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.709
to think that the Red

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:15.319
Army would come in time to liberate

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:17.689
them. There was a natural hope that

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.579
Stalin, the great leader,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.539
would emerge as the
only person in the world

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:26.460
who could defeat the Nazi war

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:27.300
machine.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.700
You could argue that this

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.220
was engaging in illusions and

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.079
wishful thinking, but what other

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.790
choice did they have?

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.730
Now, Irene Shapiro herself

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:41.860
is an amazing woman.

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:46.870
Her family came from East Galicia,

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:49.809
which was a mixed Polish, Ukrainian,

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.189
and Jewish area.

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.900
But they moved to Grudziadz,

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:57.899
a town in northwestern Poland

00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.909
near Gdansk, or Danzig,

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.269
and that's where she grew up.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.909
It was a town with a large Jewish,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.729
and it was a town with a large

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.219
German population where there were

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:12.529
very few Jews.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.239
She did not grow up speaking Yiddish.

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.879
She grew up speaking Polish.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.789
Her father was a respected music

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:21.790
teacher.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.000
By all accounts, it was a very

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.010
happy family.

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.364
But with the rising tide of Nazism

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.499
among the local Germans, the

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.989
family pulled up stakes and

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:36.389
move to Bialystok in 1938.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:39.119
Bialystok was a very different kind

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.289
of community.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:42.720
It was a

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:44.855
city where the Jews were a majority

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:48.239
of the population, where the Jews spoke

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.380
mainly Yiddish.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.978
But the family seems to have landed on its

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:54.810
feet. Her father was able to pick up

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.779
his career as a, a,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.579
as a teacher.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.820
And after a short German

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.400
occupation in September 1939,

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.500
Bialystok was taken

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.540
over by the Soviets

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.704
until June 1941

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.660
when the Germans invaded the Soviet

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:19.369
Union. And Irene Shapiro and

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:21.799
her parents and her sister found

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.820
themselves in the Bialystok ghetto.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.099
The horrors of the German occupation

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:30.859
began literally the first

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.839
day that the Germans came.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.340
Two thousand Jews were herded

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.279
into a synagogue and burned

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.340
alive. Thousands of Jewish

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.060
men were shanghaied off

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.919
the streets and shot.

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:50.699
After the first wave of terror

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:51.870
subsided,

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.129
a Bialystok ghetto was established,

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:58.780
which provided a period

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:01.070
of relative stability

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:03.688
until February 1943.

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.249
As ghettos go, the Bialystok

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:08.870
ghetto was not terrible.

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:11.659
The Judenrat enjoyed the

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:13.669
respect of most of the Jewish population.

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:16.610
It was led by an

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.256
engineer, Efraim Barasz,

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.099
who did the best he could to make

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:24.060
sure that people didn't starve, that they

00:17:24.469 --> 00:17:26.719
didn't die of epidemics.

00:17:26.959 --> 00:17:29.899
He set up many shops and enterprises,

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:33.080
and he deluded himself into thinking

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.959
that this work for the Germans

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.780
might buy time and save

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:40.140
the ghetto.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.500
There was also a resistance movement

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:44.970
in the Bialystok ghetto led by Mordechaj

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:47.139
Tenenbaum, and

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:50.079
this resistance movement,

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.369
in fact, had

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:55.300
clear ties to

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:56.480
the Judenrat.

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.559
Barasz gave them money to buy weapons.

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:03.129
Barasz had frequent conversations

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:04.130
with Tenenbaum.

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:05.960
And

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.288
the Judenrat

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.550
simply asked the resistance movement

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.609
not to do anything that might

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:18.160
give the Germans an excuse to liquidate

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:21.009
the ghetto. And Irene Shapiro

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.400
was part of that resistance organization

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:28.269
and she was

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.020
determined to go to the forests

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:33.669
and fight as a partisan.

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.510
But before that happened, the Germans

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:39.430
pounced in August

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.550
1943, the second major

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.429
action in the Bialystok ghetto.

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:47.986
And without informing Barasz,

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.489
they began to deport all the Jews.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.769
The resistance movement was taken totally

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.920
by surprise, and they were determined

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.070
to fight.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.430
Now, when this happened,

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.119
it turned out that unlike

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.700
the Warsaw ghetto, where the Jews

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:09.097
supported the fighters, where the

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.609
Jews built seven hundred fifty

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:13.610
bunkers.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:15.844
In fact, it was the civilian support

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.389
for the uprising in the Warsaw ghetto,

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.089
the hundreds of bunkers the Jews built,

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.819
which enabled the uprising to last for

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:25.869
30 days.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.599
In Bialystok, none of this happened.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:30.050
The young people

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.119
who were in the resistance were totally

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:35.120
isolated.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:37.576
The Jews did not support

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:39.950
them.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.789
They in fact believed

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:44.456
in German promises that they would

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:46.420
be sent to work.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:50.019
And Irene Shapiro found herself

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:51.847
holding a grenade and not knowing

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:53.920
what to do with it.

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:56.445
And a German soldier actually comes

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.358
over, gently takes the

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:02.019
grenade from her hand and sends

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:04.269
her over to her mother and father,

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.580
who are waiting to be loaded onto a

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:07.540
train.

00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:10.489
Some other young people fight back,

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:12.358
but within a day, the resistance

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:15.499
in the Bialystok ghetto was stamped out.

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.799
The train that the Germans put Irene

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.499
Shapiro's family on is divided

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:22.760
into two sections.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:24.240
One section is

00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:28.459
detached when the train comes

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:30.949
to Treblinka and the Jews there

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.179
are gassed.

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.429
In fact, this was the last killing in

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:35.990
Treblinka, in mid-August

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:39.620
1943, two weeks after the

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:41.250
uprising.

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:44.150
And once these Bialystok Jews were killed

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:47.299
and the camp was

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.299
dissolved, the cars

00:20:50.300 --> 00:20:51.950
that Irene Shapiro

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:54.910
happened to be in went to Majdanek,

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:57.435
and Irene Shapiro and her

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.549
mother were separated from their father.

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:04.999
They were, they went through

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.580
all the horrors of Majdanek, but luckily

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:10.489
they were deported to another labor

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:12.700
camp before all the Jews in Majdanek

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.151
were killed in November

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:17.130
1943.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:20.469
Irene Shapiro survived these

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:23.931
labor camps, lived to see the

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:27.399
liberation and then made

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:30.039
a life for herself in the United States

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:31.429
as a teacher in Bronx

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:34.560
High School of Science.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.289
And she also had a family.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.390
The, the song that she wrote

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.079
is written in Russian.

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:45.999
It's not written in Yiddish.

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:49.239
Yiddish was not Irene Shapiro's native

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.370
language, but Russian wasn't either.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:54.639
And again, I think it shows this

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:58.399
desire, this, this

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:01.450
tendency to

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:04.779
put one's hopes in

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:05.907
the power of the Soviet

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:08.360
Union.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:11.379
I want to end this

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:14.439
talk by saying that we should

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:16.869
judge this song not by

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:18.193
its literary or its musical

00:22:19.450 --> 00:22:22.119
merit, because it doesn't have

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:23.789
a lot of literary merit.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:26.739
And the song is simply the

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:29.229
kind of tune that you could hear

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:31.469
in any Soviet wartime movie.

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:34.430
But we should judge this song by another

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:35.720
criteria.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:38.529
It's an example of spiritual

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:40.870
and cultural resistance, in the

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:42.501
widest sense of the term that

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:45.820
when the Germans try to crush you,

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:48.580
when the Germans try to reduce you

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.415
to a person without agency, without

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:54.129
hope, without initiative, singing

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.549
is one way to fight back,

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:59.529
to say no matter where I am

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:01.591
and no matter how terrible my situation

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.509
might be, I can still sing,

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.100
I can still write.

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:07.636
And that reminds me that

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.260
on one level, at least, I'm still

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:12.220
a human being.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:13.500
Thank you.

